Subject: [PATCH] pppd: use a fresh CCP identifier after receive-state replacement

The CCP addci callback allocates the proposed kernel receive state on each
Configure-Request, including timer retries.  Reusing the identifier allows
an Ack already queued for the previous state to open CCP after that state
was replaced.  Linux then ignores the current Ack because CCP is already
up, leaving the new decompressor stopped.

Advance the existing FSM request identifier in CCP's retransmit callback,
before rebuilding and sending the request.  Queued replies for earlier
allocations fail the normal FSM identifier check.  This applies to all CCP
compression methods whose first receive option is allocated by addci;
initial requests, retry limits, peer-request replies and other control
protocols keep their existing behavior.

--- a/pppd/ccp.c
+++ b/pppd/ccp.c
@@ -216,6 +216,7 @@
 static void ccp_resetci (fsm *);
 static int  ccp_cilen (fsm *);
 static void ccp_addci (fsm *, u_char *, int *);
+static void ccp_retransmit (fsm *);
 static int  ccp_ackci (fsm *, u_char *, int);
 static int  ccp_nakci (fsm *, u_char *, int, int);
 static int  ccp_rejci (fsm *, u_char *, int);
@@ -239,7 +240,7 @@
     NULL,
     NULL,
     NULL,
-    NULL,
+    ccp_retransmit,
     ccp_extcode,
     "CCP"
 };
@@ -705,6 +706,18 @@
 	+ (go->mppe? CILEN_MPPE: 0)
 #endif
         ;
+}
+
+/*
+ * A retry rebuilds the kernel receive state in ccp_addci().  Give it a new
+ * identifier so a queued Ack for the retired state cannot open CCP before
+ * the replacement has been initialized by its own Ack.  RFC 1661 permits
+ * changing the identifier on retransmission; the FSM keeps its retry budget.
+ */
+static void
+ccp_retransmit(fsm *f)
+{
+    f->reqid = ++f->id;
 }
 
